Corrosion is a spontaneous process that undermines the structure of metal and alloys on account of chemical or electrochemical reaction with their surrounding environment, resulting in the costs up to $3\%$ of the gross domestic production of an industrialized country [1-4]. This grim situation motivates researchers to continually design effective strategies to hold back or slow down the natural process associated with the corrosion phenomena [5]. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) as a new class of porous materials are constructed by metal ions or clusters with organic linkers and have attracted special attention [6-8]. Besides exploiting the applications of MOFs involving catalysis [9, 10], gas storage [11] and separation [12], drug delivery [13], and sensor [14], few pioneering reports have been done on anticorrosion systems using MOFs. For example, Li et al built an anticorrosion coating system with self-healing function based on ZIF-7 nanoparticles [15]. Liu et al successfully constructed ZIF-8 coatings on Al substrates with an excellent anticorrosion performance [16]. In our previous research, CTAB@HKUST-1 thin films were proven to have good metallic corrosion protection performance [17]. All these work demonstrated MOFs materials are potentially qualified candidates for metallic corrosion protection. Unfortunately, the single MOF is not very good for long-term anticorrosion effect due to its three-dimensional pore structures. Therefore, it is still a challenge to build MOFs-based anticorrosion coatings with long-term corrosion resistance.
To improve the anticorrosion performance of MOFs material, the efficient strategy to design MOFs-based composite materials is developed. MOF-polymer composites as one of MOFs-based materials have been prepared in various synthetic approaches [18, 19], such as polymerization within nanochannels of MOFs [20, 21], polymerizations of ligands [22, 23], utilization of polymer-tethered ligands [24, 25], introduction of polymer chains into nanochannels of MOFs [19, 26]. For example, Qiu’s group reported the electropolymerization of aniline inside HKUST-1 [27]. Wang et al intertwined MOF crystals on carbon cloth with polyaniline (PANI) to achieve high-performance supercapacitors [28]. MOF-polymer
PANI, consisting of conjugated electronic structures, has received much attention in anticorrosion applications because of its non-toxicity, ease of synthesis, good adhesion and good environmental stability [29-31]. The protection efficiency of PANI has been confirmed by means of coatings and the corrosion inhibitor. In the past few decades, researchers have reported lots of anticorrosion coatings based on PANI and its derivatives [32, 33]. Ram et al demonstrated that PANI with nanodiamonds composite materials are good corrosion inhibitor for metals and their alloys owing to its chain conformation and electronic properties [34]. Recently, Ramezanzadeh et al constructed PANI/GO nanocomposites to enhance the thermal stability, barrier properties and activity inhibition of epoxy resin (EP) coatings [35]. However, PANI usually has poor dispersion in water and organic solvents, which greatly limits its application in the field of corrosion protection [36, 37]. In addition, it is not practical to disperse PANI into the solution for corrosion protection in actual applications. Therefore, we explored well-defined pores of MOFs as the container to load the corrosion inhibitor PANI. In this study, MIL-101, due to its excellent water stability, acid resistance, and large pores [38], was chosen as a container to incorporate PANI [39-41]. We described a simple and convenient strategy to encapsulate PANI into the pores of the MOF (PANI $@$ MIL-101) by aniline in-situ polymerization for achieving high anticorrosion performance. The small aniline monomers of PANI corrosion inhibitor can be easily introduced into the pores of MIL-101, and structure-controlled PANI were confined in spaces of MIL-101 by aniline in-situ polymerization. The obtained PANI@MIL-101 composite materials not only improves the anticorrosion ability of MIL-101, but also solves the loading problem of corrosion inhibitor PANI. The prepared PANI $@$ MIL-101 composite material was dispersed into EP to prepare anti-corrosive coatings, denoted as PANI@MIL-101/EP coating. The anti-corrosion performance of the prepared PANI@MIL-101/EP coating was then investigated under a simulated seawater environment $(3.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ NaCl solution). Compared with the single MIL-101, PANI and EP coating systems, the unique PANI $@$ MOF coating system exhibits more
application of PANI $@$ MOF composite materials in the corrosion protection field.
## 2. EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
2.1 Chemicals
Chromium (III) nitrate nonahydrate (Titan, $99\%$ ), 1,4-benzene dicarboxylic acid (Alfa Aesar, $99\%$ ), Aniline hydrochloride (Adamas, $99\%$ ), ammonium persulfate (J&K, $98\%$ ), xylene (Adamas, $99\%$ ) and $\mathfrak{n}$ -Butanol (Adamas, $99.5\%$ ). Epoxy resin (EP) and polyamide were obtained from Hunan New Material Co., Ltd. Dimethyl ammonium fluoride, ethanol, acetone, hydrochloric acid, acetone and aqueous HF were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. All chemicals were used as received.
## 2.2 Synthesis of MIL-101
The MIL-101 was synthesized according to a reported method [42]. Typically, MIL-101 was initially synthesized by the hydrothermal reaction in a Teflon-lined autoclave of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (4.33 g, 10 mmol), 1,4-benzene dicarboxylic acid $(1.8~\mathrm{g},10~\mathrm{mmol})$ , aqueous HF $\mathrm{\cdot}0.5\ \mathrm{mL}$ , 2.5 mmol) and de-ionized water $70~\mathrm{mL})$ at $220~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $8\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the collected green powders were handled with the boiling water for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The as-synthesized MIL-101 was filtered with the glass filter and filtered product was purified twice in ethanol at reflux temperature for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The as-synthesized crystals were further purified by using DMF, hot ethanol and aqueous $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ solutions, respectively. The MIL-101 was finally obtained by drying at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ in vacuum.
2.3 Preparation of PANI@MIL-101
Aniline hydrochloride $(50~\mathrm{mg})$ ) was dissolved in water $5~\mathrm{mL}$ ), followed by ultrasonic stirring for $10\mathrm{min}$ . Then, the activated MIL-101 ( $\mathrm{50~mg}$ ) was immersed in the above solution by incipient wetness impregnation method. The uniformly dispersed reactants were placed at
MIL-101. Then, the initiator ammonium persulfate ( $\mathrm{107~mg}$ ) was dissolved in $5\mathrm{\mL}$ hydrochloric acid solution and was added dropwise to MIL-101 solution and stirred magnetically at $0~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The powder sample was isolated from the solution by centrifugation with deionized water for 3 cycles and acetone for 3 cycles. The obtained sample was denoted as PANI $@$ MIL-101. Subsequently, the PANI $@$ MIL-101 was dried under vacuum at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . For comparison, pure PANI was prepared in the same process as PANI@MIL-101without MIL-101.
2.4 Preparation of PANI $@$ MIL-101/EP coating
The magnesium alloy sheet (AZ31B) was polished with 1500 #, 2000 # sandpapers and ultrasonicated with ethanol and acetone for $20\mathrm{min}$ to remove oxide layer. The polyamide (1.4 g) and EP $(1.75\ \mathrm{\g})$ were mixed into 3 mL n-Butanol and $7\ \mathrm{mL}$ xylene solvent. Then, PANI@MIL-101 ${\sim}0.6\%$ EP) was added to the EP solution. The above solution was ultrasonically dispersed for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ until the mixture was well distributed. Subsequently, a uniformly mixed EP containing PANI@MIL-101 was coated on the magnesium alloy substrate (AZ31B) with a coater. The PANI $@$ MIL-101/EP coatings were placed in a blast drying oven for curing at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ and then at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 7 days. In contrast, the EP coating, MIL-101/EP coating and PANI/EP coating were performed by the same way.
## 3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
In the structure of MIL-101, there are two kinds of cages with internal free diameters of opening of ${\bf\tilde{\Sigma}}\mathrm{\tilde{\Sigma}}^{12}\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , while the larger cages possess both pentagonal and larger hexagonal windows with a ${\sim}14.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ by ${\sim}16\mathrm{~\AA~}$ free aperture. As shown in Fig. S1a and Fig. S1b, the molecular sizes of aniline hydrochloride and ammonium persulfate are about 4.204 $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ and 5.936 $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ , respectively. Therefore, aniline monomer can be easily absorbed into the pores of MIL-101, and ammonium persulfate can also enter the pores to initiate the self-polymerization of the monomers. As shown in Scheme 1, the aniline monomer was first adsorbed into the pores of MIL-101 under refrigerator (ca. $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After adding the initiator ammonium persulfate solution, the aniline monomers were polymerized in the pores of MIL-101 and PANI $@$ MIL-101 composites were formed. The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern of PANI@MIL-101 matched well with both that of MIL-101 and the simulated MIL-101 (Fig. 1a), assuring the stability of the MIL-101 under the polymerized condition. To prove the existence of the PANI in PANI $@$ MIL-101, Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) were measured. As shown in Fig. 1b, the main characteristic peaks of MIL-101 at $1626~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , $1509~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and $1400~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ are assigned to -(O-C-O)- groups of the dicarboxylate [43, 44]. For PANI and PANI $@$ MIL-101 composites, the adsorption bands at $1583~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1303~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be ascribed to the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ stretching vibration of quinoid rings and C-N stretching modes of PANI, respectively [45]. The adsorption band at $1240\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is related to C-H bending mode of quinoid ring, whereas the adsorption band at $805~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is attributed to C-H out-of plane bending vibration of 1,4-disubstituted aromatic rings of PANI [46]. Furthermore, a small adsorption band showing at $1240~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is associated with the protonated $\mathrm{{C-N}^{+}}$ groups of PANI [47]. For Raman spectrum (Fig. 1c), the main characteristic Raman peaks of MIL-101 appear at $866~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , $1146~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , $1458~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and $1609~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . The intensity of
sample has two obvious peaks at $1347~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1564~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , assigning to the stretching vibrations of $\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}^{+}$ fragments and C-C stretching of benzenoid rings [48]. The corresponding peaks for both MIL-101 and PANI samples are observed in PANI $@$ MIL-101 [49]. Based on the FTIR and Raman spectra, PANI $@$ MIL-101 displays both characteristic peaks of PANI and MIL-101, proving the successful self-polymerization of aniline into MIL-101. In addition, the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) results of the MIL-101, PANI and PANI@MIL-101 also verify the existence of the PANI in PANI@MIL-101 (Fig. S2).   
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was employed to characterize oxidation states of the nitrogen atoms of PANI in the PANI@MIL-101. The XPS spectra of PANI@MIL-101 show the presence of chromium, oxygen, carbon, and nitrogen elements. Particularly, the nitrogen elements in PANI $@$ MIL-101, which are not present in MIL-101, are derived from PANI (Fig. S3). As illustrated in the Fig. 1d, the N1s spectrum of PANI $@$ MIL-101 can be fitted into two distinct curves [50, 51]. One peak at $400.6\mathrm{eV}$ is assigned to protonated - $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ - (amine). The other peak at $399.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ may correspond to protonated $\mathbf{\Lambda}=\mathrm{NH}^{+}.$ - (imine). It indicated that PANI in the PANI@MIL-101 was protonated. According to previous reports [37, 52], the protonated species get easily adsorbed on the metal surface, forming an effective barrier for the diffusion of aggressive ions.
To further prove the encapsulation of PANI into pores of MIL-101, the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms of MIL-101, PANI and PANI@MIL-101 were measured. The ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms of MIL-101, PANI and PANI $@$ MIL-101 belong to Ⅰ, Ⅲ and IV isotherm characteristics (Fig. 2a), and the specific surface area of PANI has been improved after encapsulation of PANI into pores of MIL-101 [53]. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas were $2961.63\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ and $1247.09\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ -1 for MIL-101 and PANI@MIL-101, respectively, indicating that most pores of MIL-101 were filled with PANI [54]. As shown in Fig. 2b, PANI $@$ MIL-101 has a smaller pore size compared with MIL-101, attributed to the introduction of PANI into MIL-101. This result is in agreement with the results form PXRD, FTIR and Raman analysis.
The morphologies of PANI, MIL-101 and PANI@MIL-101 are shown in Fig. 3 and Fig. 4. It can be seen that MIL-101 shows an octahedron shape (Fig. 3a and Fig. 4a) and pure PANI agglomerates amorphous shapes (Fig. 3b and Fig. 4b). For PANI@MIL-101, MIL-101 keeps perfect octahedral shapes, in spite of rough surfaces aroused by encapsulation of PANI. It indicates that MIL-101 is stable and maintains the crystallinity in the synthetic process (Fig. 3c, Fig. 3d and Fig. 4c). Such stability and crystallinity ensure that the pore space of MIL-101 could be efficiently used to accommodate PANI without loss of structural integrity. In addition, the mapping images of PANI@MIL-101 show a fairly uniform distribution of N elements throughout the MIL-101 nanostructures, suggesting that the PANI molecules are located in the pores of MIL-101. The digital photos (Fig. S4) show the dispersibility of MIL-101, PANI and PANI@MIL-101 in distilled water. The PANI precipitated completely after stated for 2h. However, no precipitation was observed in the PANI@MIL-101 dispersion after 2h. The results indicated that the dispersibility of the PANI@MIL-101 in aqueous media was excellent because PANI was encapsulated into pores of MIL-101. The excellent dispersibility of PANI@MIL-101 makes the coatings denser and compact, thus preventing the diffusion of corrosive medium ( $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and Cl-) into coatings.
