A non-destructive measurement, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), is employed to evaluate the corrosion resistance and explore protection mechanism of as-prepared coatings [59-61]. Fig. 6a and Fig. 6b display the impedance and phase angle of Bode plots for the coatings immersed in the $3.5~\mathrm{wt}\%$ NaCl solution for 10 days. Generally, the corrosion resistance of the coatings can be indicated by the impedance of low frequency $(|Z|_{0.01})$ . The higher $|Z|_{0.01}$ values correspond to the higher corrosion resistance [62, 63]. The $|Z|_{0.01}$ values of PANI $@$ MIL-101/EP coating, PANI/EP coating, MIL-101/EP coating, EP coating and magnesium alloy were $3.9\times10^{8}\Omega\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{2},4.5\times10^{7}\Omega\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{2},7.4\times10^{6}\Omega\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{2},2.1\times$ $10^{4}\ \Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ and $4.6\times10^{2}\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , respectively. It is worth noting that the $|Z|_{0.01}$ value of PANI $@$ MIL-101/EP coating is 4 orders of magnitude higher than that of the EP coating, showing that PANI@MIL-101/EP coatings have surprising corrosion protection for AZ31B. The phase angle of bode plots also reveal two time constants. The one at the high frequency region can be assigned to the capacitance and resistance of the coating. The other at low frequency comes from the contribution of the electric double layer capacitance of the interface bubble portion and the polarization resistance of the base metal corrosion reaction. According to the previous reports, the ideal phase angle (-θ) of the coating is $-90^{\circ}$ , which represents that the coating is a pure non-conductive capacitor [64-66]. Clearly, the phase angle of PANI@MIL-101/EP coating is close to $-90^{\circ}$ over a wide frequency range, indicating that this coating is almost equivalent to an insulating layer with large resistance and small capacitance. In contrast, the phase angle of PANI/EP coating, MIL-101/EP coating and EP coating is lower of different coatings are shown in Fig. 6c, d, e, f, and the larger capacitive arc in the Nyquist diagram denotes the better corrosion resistance [67-69]. The AZ31B had the smallest semicircle diameter of the capacitive arc among these coatings. In addition, with the addition of MIL-101, PANI and PANI@MIL-101 in EP, semicircle diameter of coatings will become larger. The PANI@MIL-101/EP coating exhibits the largest loop radius, confirming that this coating displays the best anticorrosion performance. In addition, the capacitive loop radius of other samples is in the following order: PANI/EP coating $>$ MIL-101/EP coating $>$ EP coating. These variations agree very well with the results of the Tafel potentiodynamic polarization. The above results indicate that the strategy of encapsulating PANI in MIL-101 pores can largely enhance the anticorrosion performance of PANI@MIL-101/EP coatings.
