To better understand the corrosion resistance and protection mechanism of ongoing corrosion, EIS spectra of the PANI $@$ MIL-101/EP coating after immersion in $3.5~\mathrm{wt}\%$ NaCl solution for different periods were obtained. Fig. 7a displays the bode plots of the PANI $@$ MIL-101/EP coating, and Fig. 7b reflects the (log $(|Z|/\Omega\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}))_{0.01}$ Hz from the bode plots of the PANI@MIL-101/EP coating during the whole immersion time. The $\mathbf{\left|\boldsymbol{Z}\right|}_{0.01}$ of PANI@MIL-101/EP coating gradually decreased from $1.1\times10^{9}\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ to $9.7\times10^{7}\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ during 20 days’ immersion. In addition, it can be seen that the diameter of the capacitive arc also reduced during 20 days’ immersion (Fig. 7c and Fig. 7d). The drop in the impedance of the bode diagram platform and capacitive arc may be caused by the infiltration of corrosive media [64, 70]. Surprisingly, the $|\mathrm{Z}|_{0.01}$ of PANI $@$ MIL-101/EP coatings rebounded after 20 days’ immersion and increased to $2.73\times10^{8}\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ on the ${30}^{\mathrm{th}}$ day (Fig. 7b), and the diameter of the capacitive arc in the Nyquist diagram also became larger. This peculiar phenomenon is attributed to the addition of PANI@MIL-101 anticorrosive composites [71, 72]. When PANI@MIL-101/EP coating was attacked by corrosive media $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ), the PANI within pores of MIL-101 can be continuously released, by which the corrosive coating is slowly repaired and the anticorrosison performance is improved. In order to further prove the controllable release of PANI, we performed the EIS of different coatings with artificial scratches. As shown in Fig. S5, |Z|0.01 values of the EP, PANI and MIL-101 coatings with artificial scratches are decreased when they are soaked for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . However, the $|Z|_{0.01}$ value of PANI@MIL-101/EP coating is increased after soaked for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ , which indirectly verifies the release of PANI. The results also indicate that the corrosion resistance of PANI@MIL-101/EP coating is improved when the immersion time is from 20 days to 30 days. After 30 days’ immersion, the $|Z|_{0.01}$ of the PANI $@$ MIL-101/EP coating gradually decreased, and the impedance became stable after 40 days’ immersion. It is noteworthy that until 50 days of immersion, the impedance resistance value of the coating still reached to $3.6\times10^{7}~\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . Also, the Nyquist curve
