Fig. 8 illustrates the corrosion protection mechanism of as-prepared specimens. For EP coating systems, EP coating can be easily attacked by corrosive media ( $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and Cl ) because there are lots of defects and holes that facilitate the formation of corrosion channels generated in the curing process (Fig. S6a) [63, 73]. It is well known that additives could help organic coating enhance the impermeability, reduce defects and holes, and prevent the penetration of the corrosive medium ( $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and Cl-). As shown in Fig. S6b and Fig. 8b, in MIL-101/EP coating systems, the morphology of the coating became relatively dense with the addition of MIL-101 by comparison with EP coating. The addition of MIL-101 to the EP coating can enhance the coating's resistance to penetration and barrier properties (Fig. 8b).
coating and MIL-101/EP coating, PANI could completely compensate for the holes and defects generated by EP during the curing, and blocks the corrosion channel invaded by the corrosion medium (Fig. S6c and Fig. 8c). Thus, PANI/EP coatings have better corrosion resistance than that of EP and MIL-101/EP coatings. In order to enhance sustainability of PANI and overcome PANI’s solubility problems, we encapsulated PANI into the pores of the MIL-101 to successfully prepare PANI@MIL-101 composite materials. As expected, PANI@MIL-101/EP coatings exhibit better anticorrosion performance than that of EP, MIL-101/EP and PANI/EP coatings. When corrosive medium starts to attack the PANI@MIL-101/EP coating, the coating exhibits more significantly physical barrier function because it is more compact helpful to reduce defects compared to other coatings. Additionally, MIL-101 can extend the length of the diffusion path, and play a role in blocking corrosive media. Furthermore, PANI is an effective corrosion inhibitor for metal, which is beneficial to improve the corrosion resistance. The central atom N of PANI has an uncommon electron pair to form a valence bond with the empty $d$ orbital of the metal surface, and PANI molecules could be adsorbed on the metal surface to form a layer of hydrophobic adsorption [74-76]. More importantly, encapsulation of PANI within the pores of MIL-101 can control the leakage of PANI corrosion inhibitor and prevent the penetration of the corrosive medium ( $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and Cl-). Therefore, the addition of PANI $@$ MIL-101 into the EP coating can delay the penetration rate of corrosive substance, extend the length of the diffusion path and provide better penetration resistance. Moreover, corrosion morphologies were further used to demonstrate the long-term anticorrosion performance (Fig. S8). After the different coating systems were immersed in corrosive mediums for 100 days, a large number of corrosion
