## 4. CONCLUSIONS
In conclusion, we demonstrated a facile strategy to prepare a new anticorrosion material PANI@MIL-101, by encapsulating PANI polymers into pores of the MOF with in-situ polymerization. The PANI@MIL-101 composite is dispersed in EP to prepare anti-corrosive coatings in order to test its anticorrosion properties. Experimental results reveal that PANI@MIL-101/EP has long-term metallic anticorrosion performance. PANI@MIL-101 composite makes the coating denser, which helps to fill the defects and holes generated during the curing of the EP and then improve the anti-penetration ability. On the other hand, PANI is an effective corrosion inhibitor for metal, which is beneficial to corrosion resistance. In addition, self-polymerization of aniline into pores of MIL-101 not only improves the loading of PANI but also controls the length of PANI molecular chains. This work creates a new avenue for developing MOFs-based coatings application in metallic anticorrosion.
